# rlvenuti.msg - Carlo Venuti, Mordino capo of Virgin Street (New Reno)
# Plain ASCII only: the English Fallout 2 fonts have no umlauts or typographic quotes.

# Act 1: the neighborhood fee
{100}{}{(A man in a pinstripe suit is sitting at your bar as if he owns it.) Carlo Venuti. I look after Virgin Street for the Desperado. The street is quiet because somebody pays for the quiet. Two hundred a week, on top of whatever you give your godfather. Call it a neighborhood fee.}
{101}{}{(A man in a pinstripe suit is sitting at your bar as if he owns it.) Carlo Venuti. Big Jesus says you're family now. Family pays family prices: two hundred a week, for the street. Think of me as your uncle.}
{110}{}{Two hundred. Fine.}
{111}{}{[Barter] A hundred. The Garter brings customers to the whole street.}
{112}{}{[Speech] I'll take this up with Big Jesus himself. Fights on Virgin Street scare off the gamblers.}
{113}{}{I'm made in another family. Take it up with them.}
{114}{}{No.}
{115}{}{We're done here.}
{116}{}{Fee?}
{120}{}{(He counts the first two hundred and smiles.) See? Neighbors.}
{121}{}{(He looks at you for a long time.) A hundred. You bargain like a Wright. I'll remember that.}
{122}{}{(Two days later Big Jesus sends a bottle and a message: no fee, no fights. Venuti delivers the message himself. He doesn't smile.)}
{123}{}{(Venuti's jaw tightens.) Your family. Fine. But the street remembers who brought other families onto it.}
{124}{}{(Venuti stands up and buttons his jacket.) Then the street isn't quiet anymore.}

# Act 4: the night of the long knives
{200}{}{(Venuti isn't sitting this time, and neither are the two men behind him.) This is the last conversation. After this, we stop talking.}
{210}{}{(Venuti looks older.) Big Jesus is dead. The Desperado is a hole in the street. The ones who are left want to know who profited. You did. So this is the last conversation.}
{211}{}{And you went over my head to Big Jesus. People on this street still laugh about it. I don't.}
{220}{}{[Speech] Let's sit down in the Desperado, like people who have something to lose.}
{221}{}{[Speech] (Take the dealer's confession to Orville Wright tonight.)}
{222}{}{Here's $1500. Let's forget this happened.}
{223}{}{Then make me family. For real.}
{224}{}{Get out of my house.}
{230}{}{(At the Desperado you drink until dawn with men who hate you. When you leave, the fee is gone and Virgin Street is divided, as it always was. Nobody bleeds tonight.)}
{231}{}{(Orville Wright reads the dealer's confession and folds it very carefully. Two nights later the Wright boys walk into the Desperado. Venuti is not seen again. Neither are you, as far as anyone can say.)}
{232}{}{(He takes the money without counting it.) Smart. The fee is gone. Next time you want to be smart, be smart earlier.}
{233}{}{(Venuti laughs and embraces you.) Family. The Garter sells what the Desperado sells now, in every house you own. Big Jesus will be very pleased. The Wrights will be something else.}
{234}{}{(Venuti steps back. His men don't.) Remember that you said that.}
{235}{}{...}

# Look
{300}{}{A man in a pinstripe suit with very clean fingernails.}
{301}{}{Carlo Venuti, Mordino capo of Virgin Street. He collects for the Desperado and remembers every name that didn't pay.}
